1
00:00:04,630 --> 00:00:12,400
Document a visit — Patients ▸ Patient History — Read the chart, record what you found, and know what the patient will see.

2
00:00:17,887 --> 00:00:28,415
Patients ▸ Patient History is the longitudinal chart — every visit, not just today’s.

3
00:00:28,415 --> 00:00:35,418
Opening a patient gives you the whole record on one set of tabs.

4
00:00:35,418 --> 00:00:48,899
The Patient Chart tab carries demographics, medical history and allergies — read the allergies first, always.

5
00:00:48,899 --> 00:00:58,227
Diagnoses is the active problem list — what this patient is being treated for.

6
00:00:58,227 --> 00:01:08,837
Records holds the notes from previous visits, newest first.

7
00:01:08,837 --> 00:01:17,348
Edit Patient Chart is where today’s findings go, grouped into tabs.

8
00:01:17,348 --> 00:01:25,833
Physical takes the measurements you just did — they chart over time automatically.

9
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:36,778
Allergies and history live here. What you add is what every other clinician sees at the next visit.

10
00:01:36,778 --> 00:01:42,918
Saved to the chart — one record, visible to the whole care team immediately.

11
00:01:42,918 --> 00:01:50,796
And to the patient: records, diagnoses and prescriptions appear in their portal, so write for both readers.

12
00:01:52,555 --> 00:02:07,095
What you just did

13
00:02:07,785 --> 00:02:16,785
Health | Efficiency | Growth — More in the Getting Started series — the next video is linked on screen.
